Level 1 – Assisted reading and writing

1. Short summary (for Michiel)
   Researchers use OpenWebUI with OpenRouter as a safe reading and writing buddy for public and non-sensitive text. It builds basic AI habits without touching documents, data, or tools.

2. Purpose of this level
   For researchers: make AI feel normal and useful for everyday text work.
   For the lectorate: low-risk visible improvement in mails, abstracts, and idea generation.

3. What researchers actually do at this level
   Use one chosen model in chat for questions about topics or courses.
   Paste short public texts (calls, articles, blogs) for summaries and simplification.
   Draft or improve mails, abstracts, announcements, LinkedIn posts.
   Brainstorm research questions, angles, and simple outlines.

4. Researcher goals (learning / behaviour)
   Feels comfortable opening OpenWebUI for small text tasks.
   Asks specific questions instead of “do my work”.
   Edits AI output so it still sounds like their own voice.
   Can give two examples where AI clearly helped.

5. Criteria: when is someone at this level?
   Has used OpenWebUI in at least three real work situations.
   Can show one mail or abstract that AI helped improve and explain their edits.
   Can explain in simple words what AI can and cannot do at this level.
   Follows basic rule: no confidential data; always review before sending.

6. Adoption challenges (for researchers)
   Feeling AI use is cheating or not academic.
   Not knowing what a good prompt looks like.
   Tendency to copy-paste AI text without thinking.

How we help them: one short intro with good vs bad prompts; a few before/after examples from our own mails and abstracts; a simple “when to use / when not to use” cheat sheet.

7. Time and support needed
   Time per researcher: one short workshop plus about one hour of practice; after that used during normal work.
   Support from lab: starter prompts, one-page guideline, quick help if someone gets stuck.
   Who can support: you plus one peer champion per team.

8. Infrastructure and data scope
   Tools used: OpenWebUI basic chat with OpenRouter models only.
   Data types: public and non-sensitive text only; no partner documents or internal datasets.
   Dependencies: OpenWebUI running; OpenRouter key configured; user accounts.

9. Risks and how we control them
   Main risks: hallucinated facts in mails, generic “AI voice”, mild plagiarism.
   Controls: clear rule to always edit and check; no blind copy-paste; no new facts without a source; no sensitive data in prompts.

10. Tangible outputs and impact
    Better structured mails, abstracts, and course communication.
    More and clearer written ideas for projects and proposals.
    Staff and students feel less stuck when starting a text.

11. Metrics and indicators
    Usage metrics: number of users and chats per month.
    Outcome metrics: a few documented before/after examples per year.
    Example KPI: at least 60 percent of staff have used OpenWebUI for three or more real work tasks at this level.

12. Path to the next level
    What is missing: AI is not yet working with their own longer notes or project texts; no document sets, no citations.
    Natural next step: Level 2, where they bring in their own notes and drafts and let AI help structure and improve them.

