Level 2 – Working with your own texts

1. Short summary (for Michiel)
   Researchers use OpenWebUI on their own notes, drafts, and emails to clean up and structure text. Still simple, but now connected to real project material.

2. Purpose of this level
   For researchers: reduce overwhelm by turning messy notes into clear outlines and sections.
   For the lectorate: faster, more structured proposals, reflections, and internal documents.

3. What researchers actually do at this level
   Paste their own notes, interview snippets, mail threads, and half-finished paragraphs into chat.
   Ask for summaries, outlines, and alternative formulations of their material.
   Use one or two simple prompt patterns such as “turn these notes into an outline” or “rewrite this for partner X”.
   Iterate between AI suggestions and their own edits.

4. Researcher goals (learning / behaviour)
   Use AI on their own text, not just public examples.
   Turn chaos (mixed notes, languages, fragments) into a usable structure.
   Produce at least one real document (for example proposal section, reflection, or memo) clearly improved by AI support.
   Understand what input works well and what is too messy or sensitive.

5. Criteria: when is someone at this level?
   Has used AI on their own project material in at least two different documents.
   Can show one example where AI turned raw notes into a usable outline or draft.
   Can explain what they gave to AI and what they kept or changed.
   Respects rule: no sensitive partner data or contracts at this level unless explicitly cleared.

6. Adoption challenges (for researchers)
   Uncertainty about what is safe to paste into chat.
   Mixed language and shorthand notes leading to weird output.
   Fear of losing their own voice when AI rewrites strongly.
   Extra friction of copying, pasting, and organising texts instead of just typing in Word.

How we help them: simple data-safety rules; examples of “good input” vs “too messy” input; ready-made prompts for “notes to outline” and “rewrite in my tone”; one short guided exercise on their own text.

7. Time and support needed
   Time per researcher: one focused session where they try it on their own project text; after that, used whenever they have messy notes.
   Support from lab: template prompts; short checklist “what to paste / what not to paste”; availability for quick questions.
   Who can support: you; one or two early adopters who can show their workflow.

8. Infrastructure and data scope
   Tools used: same OpenWebUI chat with OpenRouter models; no complex RAG yet.
   Data types: their own non-sensitive project notes and drafts; no bulk partner document uploads; no large PDFs.
   Dependencies: Level 1 habits; clear data rules.

9. Risks and how we control them
   Main risks: losing nuance from raw notes; over-rewriting in AI voice; accidental paste of sensitive content.
   Controls: side-by-side comparison of original vs AI version; mandatory manual edit; data guideline and quick reminder in the UI or workshop.

10. Tangible outputs and impact
    Cleaner, more structured drafts for proposals, reflections, and memos.
    Less time lost on sorting old notes and emails.
    Researchers feel more in control of their own text backlog.

11. Metrics and indicators
    Usage metrics: number of people who used AI on their own project texts.
    Outcome metrics: count of documents where AI-assisted restructuring was used.
    Example KPI: at least 40 percent of staff have used AI to turn their own notes or drafts into a clearer outline or section in at least one project.

12. Path to the next level
    What is missing: AI still sees only what they paste in; no persistent project document sets; no searching across many files.
    Natural next step: Level 3, where they build small document collections and “talk” to them with citations.

