Level 3 – Talking with documents

1. Short summary (for Michiel)
   Researchers use OpenWebUI and RAG to work with sets of project documents. AI can search, compare, and summarise across PDFs, PPTs, survey exports, and transcripts with citations.

2. Purpose of this level
   For researchers: replace endless scrolling in PDFs with targeted questions and cited answers.
   For the lectorate: faster orientation in complex document sets and more traceable background sections in reports.

3. What researchers actually do at this level
   Create a small document set per project (for example 3–50 files: PDFs, PPTs, Word, CSV exports).
   Upload files with simple naming and grouping.
   Ask detailed questions and read answers with citations to specific documents and pages.
   Compare versions such as policy vs practice, wave 1 vs wave 2, or different partner reports.
   Use transcripts from lectures, webinars, or meetings as part of the same document set.

4. Researcher goals (learning / behaviour)
   Build and actually use a mini knowledge base around their project.
   Quickly find “where do we say X?” or “who mentioned Y?” across the set.
   Write at least one short cited section (context, related work, or findings summary) based on RAG queries.
   Understand the limits of RAG (it is not magic, depends on what you upload).

5. Criteria: when is someone at this level?
   Has created at least one project document set or knowledge base in OpenWebUI.
   Has asked and answered concrete questions using that set with citations.
   Can show one paragraph or page in a report that came from this process and point to the sources.
   Follows agreed rules for which documents are allowed, especially partner material.

6. Adoption challenges (for researchers)
   Initial friction collecting, cleaning, and uploading files.
   Unclear practices around naming and structuring document sets.
   Frustration if early queries do not hit the right passages.
   Worry about uploading partner documents into “the system”.
   Feeling that this preparation is extra work next to just reading.

How we help them: a simple “build a knowledge base” checklist; example project they can copy; naming conventions and folder suggestions; clear rule which partner docs are allowed and how access is managed.

7. Time and support needed
   Time per researcher: two to four hours once per project to gather and upload documents; after that, questions and summaries happen during normal work.
   Support from lab: help with first setup; tuning of RAG settings if needed; example queries.
   Who can support: you and one or two people comfortable with the document and RAG settings.

8. Infrastructure and data scope
   Tools used: OpenWebUI document upload and RAG; possibly Qdrant as external vector store if you choose.
   Data types: project documents, internal reports, selected partner material, transcripts; all inside project-scoped collections.
   Dependencies: Level 2 habits; configured RAG backend; some storage space.

9. Risks and how we control them
   Main risks: wrong or incomplete citations; misinterpretation of partner documents; storing documents in the wrong project space.
   Controls: citations always visible; simple access rules per project; clear labelling of what is and is not in each collection; manual review of key outputs.

10. Tangible outputs and impact
    Cited summaries and comparisons for reports and proposals.
    Faster onboarding of new students and staff into an existing project.
    Less re-reading and re-locating information across many files.

11. Metrics and indicators
    Usage metrics: number of active project document sets or knowledge bases.
    Outcome metrics: number of outputs (sections, memos) that were produced using RAG with citations.
    Example KPI: at least one active document set and one cited AI-generated summary or comparison per strategic project per year.

12. Path to the next level
    What is missing: AI still mainly reads documents; it does not yet call tools or automate workflows.
    Natural next step: Level 4, where agents and tools start to run small analyses and repeatable tasks on top of this information.

