Level 4 – Tools, agents and workflows

1. Short summary (for Michiel)
   Researchers start using prebuilt agents and tools in OpenWebUI that can call code, web search, and external services. AI becomes a semi-automated coworker for repetitive and structured tasks.

2. Purpose of this level
   For researchers: offload recurring, boring tasks to an agent instead of doing them manually each time.
   For the lectorate: more consistent outputs and visible time savings on real processes.

3. What researchers actually do at this level
   Use shared agents such as a resilience helper, survey cleaner, CSRD explainer, or report drafter.
   Trigger tools that run small Python code, web searches, or HTTP calls to n8n, Supabase, or other services.
   Reuse the same agent weekly for tasks like summarising survey waves, drafting radar narratives, or generating partner-specific summaries.
   Give feedback to improve prompts and tool behaviour.

4. Researcher goals (learning / behaviour)
   Regularly delegate at least one recurring task to an agent or tool.
   Understand in simple terms what the agent does and what it is allowed to call.
   Explain how using the agent saves time or improves quality in their project.
   Stay in the loop as editor and owner of the final result.

5. Criteria: when is someone at this level?
   Has used at least one shared agent or tool in three or more real work moments.
   Can describe what input they give and how they check the output.
   Can explain which tools or external services the agent is allowed to use.
   Has one documented case where a task went from “takes hours” to “takes minutes”.

6. Adoption challenges (for researchers)
   Feeling that this is too technical or “for programmers”.
   Fear of breaking something when tools touch data or APIs.
   Dependence on one expert to configure and maintain agents.
   Not knowing which agent to use for which task.
   Risk of over-automation and forgetting to think critically.

How we help them: short live demo of a few agents on real project data; one-page catalogue “which agent for what”; simple request channel for new or adjusted agents; office hours to try agents together.

7. Time and support needed
   Time per researcher: one focused demo session and about one hour guided practice; after that, agents used during normal work.
   Support from lab: you or a small tech group to build and maintain agents; documentation for each agent; light training.
   Who can support: you; maybe one n8n or Supabase person; an interested student assistant.

8. Infrastructure and data scope
   Tools used: OpenWebUI tools and functions; possibly pipelines; external services like n8n, Supabase, Qdrant, or other APIs.
   Data types: project and partner data that the agents are explicitly allowed to access, following agreed rules.
   Dependencies: Levels 2 and 3; basic tool and pipeline configuration; API connections set up.

9. Risks and how we control them
   Main risks: hidden complexity in tools; wrong calls to external services; quietly propagating errors; overtrust in automation.
   Controls: clear description of each agent’s scope; role-based access for powerful tools; logging of tool calls; simple tests before using agents in live partner work.

10. Tangible outputs and impact
    Less manual copy-paste work; faster recurring analyses and summaries.
    Consistent structure and wording across reports and resilience scans.
    First clear examples of “this AI setup saves us X hours per month”.

11. Metrics and indicators
    Usage metrics: number of active agents and tool calls; number of users per agent.
    Outcome metrics: documented time saved for a few key workflows.
    Example KPI: each strategic project uses at least one shared agent, with one documented “before / after” time comparison.

12. Path to the next level
    What is missing: agents and tools exist but full data pipelines and dashboards are still mostly separate.
    Natural next step: Level 5, where AI agents, data stores, n8n flows, and reporting pipelines are wired into end-to-end lab infrastructure.

