Level 5 – Full stack lab integration

1. Short summary (for Michiel)
   OpenWebUI, OpenRouter, n8n, Supabase, Qdrant, and dashboards work together as one system. Researchers use end-to-end AI-supported pipelines, not just individual chats or agents.

2. Purpose of this level
   For researchers: a stable environment where data, AI, and reporting are connected, so they can focus on research questions.
   For the lectorate: a reusable research infrastructure that speeds up new projects and strengthens the lab’s position.

3. What researchers actually do at this level
   Use shared “lab assistants” that read from databases, knowledge bases, and dashboards.
   Trigger end-to-end flows via agents or buttons (for example from raw data to first report draft).
   Participate in the design and refinement of a pipeline so it fits their research design.
   Look at simple logs or dashboards to understand what the pipeline did and where.

4. Researcher goals (learning / behaviour)
   Use at least one end-to-end pipeline in a real project, not just a demo.
   Understand the main steps from data source to AI to output well enough to explain them.
   Provide feedback and small adjustments so the pipeline becomes reusable for future work.
   Stay responsible for interpretation and final reporting.

5. Criteria: when is someone at this level?
   Has used a pipeline that connects data, AI, and reporting in at least one live project.
   Can sketch the main steps on paper: where the data comes from, what AI does, where the result goes.
   Follows governance rules for data, access, and approvals.
   Contributes at least one lesson or improvement back into the shared setup.

6. Adoption challenges (for researchers)
   Coordination with IT, data owners, and partners.
   Fear that the pipeline becomes a black box they cannot control.
   Questions about data ownership, access rights, and long-term maintenance.
   Change fatigue from “another system” next to existing tools.

How we help them: a clear reference architecture picture; one flagship project as example; joint sessions with IT and researchers walking through a real pipeline; simple non-technical documentation per pipeline.

7. Time and support needed
   Time per researcher: some extra time in initial design and feedback; later the pipeline should save time on each cycle.
   Support from lab: you plus a small technical team for n8n, Supabase, Qdrant, and deployments; regular maintenance.
   Who can support: you; one infra-oriented colleague; maybe a data engineer or advanced student.

8. Infrastructure and data scope
   Tools used: OpenWebUI front-end; OpenRouter models; n8n for orchestration; Supabase and/or other databases; Qdrant for vectors; dashboards or Quarto for reporting.
   Data types: internal and partner data in governed spaces with clear access rules.
   Dependencies: Levels 3 and 4 mature; IT and governance agreements in place.

9. Risks and how we control them
   Main risks: wrong or outdated data flowing through pipelines; unclear responsibilities; dependency on a few people; system drift over time.
   Controls: explicit ownership per pipeline; logging and monitoring; change management; documented fallbacks if AI or a service fails; regular reviews.

10. Tangible outputs and impact
    End-to-end flows from data to report that can be reused across projects.
    Faster startup of new projects; easier reuse of existing logic and structures.
    Clear stories and demos for management and partners showing lab impact.

11. Metrics and indicators
    Usage metrics: number of projects using at least one end-to-end pipeline.
    Outcome metrics: time from raw data to first report draft before vs after.
    Example KPI: at least two flagship projects per year running on the full stack with documented time savings and quality improvements.

12. Path to the next step
    What is missing: perfection; there will always be new models, tools, and governance questions.
    Natural next step: continuous improvement; refining pipelines, adding new agents, and aligning with future regulations and university strategy.
